# The network of "Primary, backup and the VPN": a company's router with
# two private circuits and a VPN to one cloud.
#
#   office 10.1.0.10 -- dc -- edge (AS 65001) ==dx1 169.254.30.0/30== cloud (AS 64512) -- vpc -- app 10.100.0.10
#                                            \==dx2 169.254.20.0/30==//
#                                             \==vpn 169.254.10.0/30==/
#
# dx1 and dx2 stand in for two AWS Direct Connect connections, vpn for a
# Site-to-Site VPN over the internet: each is a cable with a BGP session
# over it. edge has the office's eight networks, 10.1.0.0/24 to
# 10.1.7.0/24, as eight addresses on its LAN. The cloud's router is scenery,
# configured and saved, and follows a provider's documented rules (the
# prompt quotes them); edge is the learner's. vpn's addresses are the lowest
# and dx1's the highest, so when everything else about two paths is equal,
# the tie goes the wrong way (and FRR shares the traffic over two equal
# paths): the lab can't be passed by leaving things as they are. Each
# router's interfaces are named after the cable or network at the far end.
# Addresses come from the private ranges and the link-local block for
# circuits and tunnels (169.254.0.0/16), as the clouds use, and AS numbers
# from the private range (RFC 6996).
machine office
machine app
machine edge router
machine cloud router

lan dc office:eth0=10.1.0.10/24 edge:lan=10.1.0.1/24,10.1.1.1/24,10.1.2.1/24,10.1.3.1/24,10.1.4.1/24,10.1.5.1/24,10.1.6.1/24,10.1.7.1/24
link edge:dx1=169.254.30.1/30 cloud:dx1=169.254.30.2/30
link edge:dx2=169.254.20.1/30 cloud:dx2=169.254.20.2/30
link edge:vpn=169.254.10.1/30 cloud:vpn=169.254.10.2/30
lan vpc cloud:vpc=10.100.0.1/16 app:eth0=10.100.0.10/16

route office default via 10.1.0.1
route app default via 10.100.0.1

run edge frr
run cloud frr
run office web-svc
run app web-svc
